Separate approximation error from a precise solution of the wrong equations (@chemistry-solution-approximation: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-an-acid-component-ledger-and-a-water-inclusive-equilibrium.lingua#r_006257BCEF8633C87FC3DDB907") { r_00CD6128D8573946F02DAF4D5B
The full neutral-HA model distinguishes two common simplifications. First neglect water's hydroxide contribution to charge balance, so h≈[A⁻]. Component and acid relations then give h₀²+K_a,c h₀−K_a,c C=0. Its positive root can be written stably as

h₀=2K_a,c C/(K_a,c+√(K_a,c²+4K_a,c C)).

Rationalizing the usual quadratic root avoids cancellation of nearly equal positive terms. It does not restore water, nonideal activities or missing ions. At C=0 this simplified model gives zero h, revealing the loss of the water account. For C>0, the full residual evaluated at h₀ equals −K_w,c/h₀<0, so the unique full-model root h* is greater than h₀. This establishes the direction of the neglected-water error within the stated model.

A second simplification assumes dissociation is small relative to the analytical total: [HA]≈C. Along with the first approximation it gives h_s=√(K_a,c C). The no-water equation implies h₀²=K_a,c(C−h₀)<K_a,c C, so h_s>h₀. But it need not lie above or below h*: the two approximations can partially cancel or compound. A closer-looking result is not evidence that both assumptions hold.

Test approximations against a declared accuracy target. Evaluate the actual component, charge and equilibrium residuals; compare the omitted hydroxide with the relevant charge terms and the dissociated fraction with the total; and, where possible, compare with the certified full root. A mnemonic percentage threshold is not a universal error guarantee for every output. The pH change associated with a concentration ratio is −log₁₀(h_est/h*); relative concentration error and logarithmic error are different quantities.

For the original example C=4.5×10⁻⁷ mol/L, K_a,c=10⁻⁷ mol/L and K_w,c=10⁻¹⁴ (mol/L)², the complete root is h*=2×10⁻⁷ mol/L. The no-water root is ((√19−1)/2)×10⁻⁷ mol/L, about 16.03% low. The square-root result is √(9/8) times h*, between 6% and 7% high. It happens to be closer although it neglects an additional term. Neither meets a 1% concentration criterion. The exact state has [A⁻]/C=1/3 and [OH⁻]/h*=1/4, directly exposing both neglected contributions.

At the much smaller C=10⁻¹⁰ mol/L with the same coefficients, the full root satisfies 10⁻⁷<h*≤1.001×10⁻⁷ mol/L. The no-water root is below C, and √(K_a,c C) is also below 10⁻⁷ mol/L. Interpreting either as a full hydrogen concentration would incorrectly place the ideal acid solution on the basic side of the neutral point. The failure is chemical-model omission, not insufficient floating-point precision.

**Work:** use [Derive a weak-acid calculation and test its approximation](../general-chemistry/aqueous/Weak-acids-and-valid-approximations.lingua#r_1X16MN6WMFKKBA6XGFSSHZB8XP) for the existing broader weak-acid and conjugate-base assignments. Prove each inequality above, compare the residual-based certificate with the root bracket and explain why a certificate for the simplified residual does not certify the complete model. Retain the distinction among numerical truncation, uncertain coefficients and omitted physics/chemistry.

**Continue:** [Investigate an acid component ledger and a water inclusive equilibrium](Investigate-an-acid-component-ledger-and-a-water-inclusive-equilibrium.lingua#r_006257BCEF8633C87FC3DDB907).

**Return:** [Chemistry](../Chemistry.lingua#r_2NWJY1HCKPBM4T6YQSDHN8S3S0).
